     "Merge after R10-FLOW-DESIGN-POLICY (the templates cite its contracts); build beside it.",
     "Pre-study: the Lean flow pre-study, section K19.",
     "Self-test hygiene: (a) a new seat-selftest mutant in dev/audit/harnesses/r10_seat_selftests_mutants.py takes a per-file id (for example cloud_relay-1), never a new S number (the P6 convention), and sits inside the block of the instrument it tests, never appended at the file's end, so parallel additions touch different hunks; on 2026-10-21 three pull requests (R10-FLOW-RELAY-HOLD, R10-FLOW-YIELD-PROBE, R10-CI-LAYOUT's PR G) each lost a code round to a content conflict there when main gained tick_loop mutants S31-S34. (b) A self-test builds any git repository through tests/throwaway_git.py, never a hand-built git init (a pusher-reap handoff was refused for exactly that in tools/audit/seat/pr_args.sh); run python3 tests/throwaway_git.py --check before the handoff. (c) Any new git push site is registered in PUSHERS in tools/audit/seat/ref_privacy.py in the same pull request, or ref_privacy refuses it (#2582's heartbeat push did). (d) The body's Red checks section answers every check red on main's ancestry, one line each (today nightly-status), or pr-contract refuses it (standing rule 22).",
-    "Triage P-A (s4, batch 3, 2026-10-22, accepted narrowed), verbatim:\n## P-A Cloud dispatch payload size: render a stub that points at a pushed ref\n\nVERDICT: ACCEPT NARROWED -> R10-FLOW-DESIGN-STAGES (not-started, M, no `after`, files already include\ntools/audit/seat/cloud_dispatch.py). Plus a cleanup carry to R10-FLY-PRUNE-REFS (not-started).\n\nMeasured facts:\n- The ref half is already on main. R10-FLOW-DISPATCH is done. `dispatch_group()` / `--role R --group G --model M`\n  publishes PAYLOAD.json and PROMPT.md as `dispatch/<role>-<topic>` and prints the ref name. INSTRUMENTS.md says \"fire\n  the standing trigger with the printed ref\". 19 `dispatch/*` refs exist on origin.\n- The missing piece: nothing renders the short trigger body. The ccr is resent whole on every trigger update (INSTRUMENTS.md,\n  \"every trigger update resends the full ccr\"), so a standing trigger still needs a ccr whose prompt names the ref. Today\n  the only output is the full ccr or the bare ref name. No `--stub` or equivalent exists (grep of cloud_dispatch.py).\n- Volume: cloud-sessions.tsv has 35 fires on 2026-10-22. Brief plus carries of not-started roster groups: median 2.4 KB,\n  max 28.9 KB, so the saving is concentrated in the large briefs (P2b-class), not the median.\n- Not re-measured: the orchestrator's \"about 35k tokens for 11 KB\". 11 KB is about 3k tokens per copy, so 3 echoes is\n  about 9k; 35k implies roughly 12 copies. Treat the figure as unverified; the direction holds at either number.\nRef-family cleanup cost (the question asked): a stub adds no new ref, since it reuses `dispatch/*`. But\nprune_refs.py has no `dispatch/` or `brief/` rule (grep: only handoff/, verdict/, ci-probe/, archive/), so these refs are\nresidue that nothing deletes. 19 dispatch refs now, about 35 a day at the 10-22 rate, and 1 `brief/*` ref. R10-FLY-PRUNE-REFS\ncarry (ii) already guards \"dispatch/* younger than 24h\", which presumes a delete rule that is not there.\nCost: S inside an M group on the same file; one `--stub` arm, a self-test arm with null control (full ccr unchanged byte for byte),\na mutant. Benefit: orchestrator tokens per fire, 35 fires a day.\nBinding constraints: (1) the seat then reads the brief at start, not at `run`; a brief edited between the fire and the seat's\nfetch reaches the seat, which is acceptable but must be stated; (2) the stub must not carry host paths (ref_privacy, already\nenforced for the ref); (3) cloud_dispatch.py is a hot file: R10-FLOW-DESIGN-STAGES also edits it, so one owner avoids a conflict."
+    "Triage P-A (s4, batch 3, 2026-10-22, accepted narrowed), verbatim:\n## P-A Cloud dispatch payload size: render a stub that points at a pushed ref\n\nVERDICT: ACCEPT NARROWED -> R10-FLOW-DESIGN-STAGES (not-started, M, no `after`, files already include\ntools/audit/seat/cloud_dispatch.py). Plus a cleanup carry to R10-FLY-PRUNE-REFS (not-started).\n\nMeasured facts:\n- The ref half is already on main. R10-FLOW-DISPATCH is done. `dispatch_group()` / `--role R --group G --model M`\n  publishes PAYLOAD.json and PROMPT.md as `dispatch/<role>-<topic>` and prints the ref name. INSTRUMENTS.md says \"fire\n  the standing trigger with the printed ref\". 19 `dispatch/*` refs exist on origin.\n- The missing piece: nothing renders the short trigger body. The ccr is resent whole on every trigger update (INSTRUMENTS.md,\n  \"every trigger update resends the full ccr\"), so a standing trigger still needs a ccr whose prompt names the ref. Today\n  the only output is the full ccr or the bare ref name. No `--stub` or equivalent exists (grep of cloud_dispatch.py).\n- Volume: cloud-sessions.tsv has 35 fires on 2026-10-22. Brief plus carries of not-started roster groups: median 2.4 KB,\n  max 28.9 KB, so the saving is concentrated in the large briefs (P2b-class), not the median.\n- Not re-measured: the orchestrator's \"about 35k tokens for 11 KB\". 11 KB is about 3k tokens per copy, so 3 echoes is\n  about 9k; 35k implies roughly 12 copies. Treat the figure as unverified; the direction holds at either number.\nRef-family cleanup cost (the question asked): a stub adds no new ref, since it reuses `dispatch/*`. But\nprune_refs.py has no `dispatch/` or `brief/` rule (grep: only handoff/, verdict/, ci-probe/, archive/), so these refs are\nresidue that nothing deletes. 19 dispatch refs now, about 35 a day at the 10-22 rate, and 1 `brief/*` ref. R10-FLY-PRUNE-REFS\ncarry (ii) already guards \"dispatch/* younger than 24h\", which presumes a delete rule that is not there.\nCost: S inside an M group on the same file; one `--stub` arm, a self-test arm with null control (full ccr unchanged byte for byte),\na mutant. Benefit: orchestrator tokens per fire, 35 fires a day.\nBinding constraints: (1) the seat then reads the brief at start, not at `run`; a brief edited between the fire and the seat's\nfetch reaches the seat, which is acceptable but must be stated; (2) the stub must not carry host paths (ref_privacy, already\nenforced for the ref); (3) cloud_dispatch.py is a hot file: R10-FLOW-DESIGN-STAGES also edits it, so one owner avoids a conflict.",
+    "Triage (s4, 2026-10-23 07:10Z, RemoteTrigger payload echo, accepted, narrowed), verbatim:\nA stub that pins the pushed commit, not the ref name.\n\nMeasured facts: tools/audit/seat/cloud_dispatch.py already publishes dispatch/<role>-<topic> (PAYLOAD.json and PROMPT.md), but render() (:171-:205) still builds the event message from _prompt(..., brief), with the brief inlined (:167-168, \"snapshotted when this payload was rendered\"). Each trigger update resends the whole ccr, so the 11 KB brief is echoed in every update and run; the proposer's measure (~35k tokens per fire, ~140k for 4) is the orchestrator's observation, not re-measured from git.\nConstraint 1: the inlined brief is deliberate (\"a brief edited afterwards reaches no running seat\"), so a stub that names only brief/<topic> would let a later push change a running seat's brief. The stub must name the dispatch commit SHA (git fetch origin dispatch/<role>-<topic>, then read PROMPT.md at that SHA), which keeps the snapshot; never a moving ref.\nConstraint 2: the stub keeps the first-line grammar, role, head, nonce, pr, topic and FOOTER_BAN inline (a reviewer's verdict is matched to its dispatch by nonce); only the brief body moves.\nConstraint 3: the host-path refusal (HOME regex) must still run on the published PROMPT.md, since it is what the seat reads.\nConstraint 4: the seat needs git read access to origin; the ccr already lists the repository as a source and allows Bash, but one fire on the stub path must prove the seat reads the brief before the mode becomes the default.\nOrder: after DESIGN-STAGES, or put the stub in the same render change, so two PRs do not both edit render(). R10-FLOW-DISPATCH owns this file but is done, so it is not the destination.\nCost: S-M, about 40-70 lines in cloud_dispatch.py (a --stub flag or default, SHA from the dispatch push) plus self-test rows: stub payload under 1 KB with the brief absent, the SHA present, a host path in the brief still refused, and a null control that the inlined mode is byte-identical to today's. About 30k tokens saved per fire by the proposer's figures."
