    "brief": "Mechanise the two design steps (owner ruling, 2026-10-21). (1) Roster stages design and design-review, accepted by roster_edit.py set-stage and shown by the tick board. (2) Bus refs design/<group-topic> for the design note and design-verdict/<group-topic> for the reviewer's verdict, written through bus.sh like review/<N> and verdict/<N>. (3) Dispatch templates for the designer and the design reviewer that name the contract and pin the base SHA, and refuse a fixer dispatch for an eligible group with no approved design verdict. (4) A tick line listing designs owed: an eligible group whose last predecessor has dispatched and that has no design ref. (5) Two cloud roles in cloud_dispatch.py beside reviewer, fixer and probe: designer, which may push only to design/<group-topic> and opens no pull request, and design-reviewer, which may push only to design-verdict/<group-topic>; each role's text says so itself, so no brief has to override the probe role's 'send no code to any ref' (revision 14, observed on the R10-SCORES designer). Until this lands, the orchestrator runs the steps by hand on the same ref names, dispatching the probe role with that override.",
@@ -6947,7 +6948,8 @@
     "bus.sh push-verdict/confirm of a large ev/ (73 files, ~30MB, #2563 probe 7e7638c4) fails HTTP 400 (chunked POST over default http.postBuffer) and cloud_relay marks the ref .refused SILENTLY (reason not logged, tick never shows it); verdict sat 3h unposted. Fix: push with -c http.postBuffer=524288000 (set in repo config as stopgap) and log REFUSED <ref> <reason> as a tick OWED level line; probe_arm writes .refused on every non-zero rc so transient push failures are never retried: retry transients. Also cap ev/ size in the reviewer payload.",
     "Triage P-B (s4, batch 3, 2026-10-22, accepted), verbatim:\n## P-B tick_loop.sh runs stale code: freshness check\n\nVERDICT: ACCEPT -> R10-FLY-RELAY-REVIEW-FF (not-started, S, files include tools/audit/seat/tick_loop.sh and cloud_relay.sh),\nplus a forward-carry to R10-ORCH-MACHINERY's design (finding-propagation) because the same hazard moves to beatd.py.\n\nMeasured facts (origin/main tools/audit/seat/tick_loop.sh):\n- It does no self-check at all. No `git` call, no SHA, no version in the banner or the heartbeat message. Each pass runs\n  `$HERE/cloud_relay.sh pass` and `$HERE/orchestrator_tick.py` from whatever checkout it started in; only `tick_loop.sh` itself is\n  read once. So a checkout never updates and every fix to those files is invisible to a running loop.\n- The stale checkout 16a8240f1 (2026-10-22 08:11 +0200) is 506 commits behind origin/main fef4a1351; 119 touch tools/audit/seat and\n  28 touch cloud_relay.sh, tick_loop.sh or orchestrator_tick.py. The hold check landed in cfa89d24f (R10-FLOW-RELAY-HOLD).\n  A path filter on tools/audit/seat therefore cuts the restart rate from 506 to 119 candidates.\n- Ownership: no group owns it. R10-RCA-ORCH-CONTINUITY and R10-FLOW-ANDON (tick_loop.sh, both done) cover liveness (heartbeat), not\n  code freshness, so the heartbeat reads healthy while the code is old. R10-ORCH-MACHINERY (L, not-started) moves the relay to a\n  launchd-supervised beat; its brief has no clause on which checkout the beat runs, so the hazard would carry over. FLY-RELAY-REVIEW-FF\n  and R10-FLOW-AUTOTRAIN are the not-started groups on tick_loop.sh; REVIEW-FF is S and sits on the relay_start lines.\nDesign (narrower than the proposal):\n- Do not fast-forward by default. The loop is documented as a read-only driver, and orch-wt is a worktree other seats may share\n  (a dirty tree or a detached HEAD would make `merge --ff-only` fail or surprise).\n- Each pass: `git -C $HERE fetch -q origin main` (a git fetch, no API) and count `HEAD..origin/main -- tools/audit/seat`. Put\n  `code=<sha7> behind=<N>` in the TICK-PASS banner and the heartbeat message, so staleness shows off-host.\n- If behind>0 and the tree is clean and `merge --ff-only` succeeds: `bash -n` the new tick_loop.sh, then `exec bash \"$0\" \"$@\"`. Otherwise\n  print `TICK-STALE behind=N` and carry on (never exit: a stopped loop is worse than a stale one).\nNull control: a throwaway origin whose main did not move prints behind=0 and does not exec. Mutant: drop the path filter or the\n`bash -n` guard. Cost: S; one fetch per 300 s pass, 288 a day, no API.\nBinding constraints: tick_loop.sh is measured by the mutant file (new arm takes a per-file id, e.g. tick_loop-N); a new git call\nsites need no PUSHERS entry (fetch only). The exec guard matters: a broken main would crash-loop the driver, so the `bash -n`\ncheck and the single re-exec per pass are part of the carry.",
     "Triage P-C (s4, batch 4, 2026-10-23, accepted as already carried): #2635 is the second measured instance of the P15 fold. Probe landed 00:20:59Z, relayed 00:21:08Z, cloud_relay printed 'next: bus.sh confirm 2635 <review commit>' and stopped; the verdict posted only when main ran confirm by hand about 00:25Z, after the relay seat reported NOT CONFIRMED. Cause: tick_loop.sh relay_start (line 63) runs 'cloud_relay.sh pass' without --confirm, although cloud_relay.sh --confirm exists (1acdb3cdc) and logs POSTED/OWED, which orchestrator_tick.py relay_owed already prints as a level. Fix: pass --confirm in relay_start; one self-test line in tick_loop.sh (stub relay logs its args; assert 'pass --confirm'; null control: relay=absent still ticks); a per-file mutant that drops --confirm must be killed; add a cloud_relay.sh self-test case that confirm on an already posted review sha logs OWED or no-ops and never posts twice. Do not build a CONFIRM OWED board line (Option B): OWED and BUS unconfirmed already cover a failed or missing confirm. State in the PR body that --confirm replaces the orchestrator's read of the reviewer thread with the relay's nonce, head and dispatch-record checks (decided 2026-10-18).",
-    "Triage P-E (s4, 2026-10-23 03:45Z, accepted, narrowed to a git-only detector), verbatim:\nWhen a dispatched cloud seat's return ref and the PR head have not moved N hours after dispatch, report \"SEAT ENDED NO RETURN <topic> <cse>\".\n\nMeasured facts: no rule looks for a dispatched fixer with no return (orchestrator_tick.py and cloud_relay.sh: none), and the 6 h silence on #2620 is a real hole. The proposed mechanism needed RemoteTrigger list_runs, and cloud_relay.sh is bash and subagent seats lack RemoteTrigger, so it cannot be called from the pass. Detector: for each dispatched cse in the sessions record, report the line when the return ref has not moved and no PR head moved within a threshold (for example 2 h) after dispatch time; the worker_status check, if wanted, is a main-side enrichment. An added tick line in existing instruments, not a new rule or lint class.\nOpen point the fixer must close first: where cloud-sessions.tsv is written and what its created_at column holds were not found; confirm the record's shape before writing the detector.\nCost: S-M, about 40-80 lines plus a fixture with a null control (a seat that did return must not be reported). If the tick line is wanted in orchestrator_tick.py, that part belongs to R10-FLY-CAP-LINE."
+    "Triage P-E (s4, 2026-10-23 03:45Z, accepted, narrowed to a git-only detector), verbatim:\nWhen a dispatched cloud seat's return ref and the PR head have not moved N hours after dispatch, report \"SEAT ENDED NO RETURN <topic> <cse>\".\n\nMeasured facts: no rule looks for a dispatched fixer with no return (orchestrator_tick.py and cloud_relay.sh: none), and the 6 h silence on #2620 is a real hole. The proposed mechanism needed RemoteTrigger list_runs, and cloud_relay.sh is bash and subagent seats lack RemoteTrigger, so it cannot be called from the pass. Detector: for each dispatched cse in the sessions record, report the line when the return ref has not moved and no PR head moved within a threshold (for example 2 h) after dispatch time; the worker_status check, if wanted, is a main-side enrichment. An added tick line in existing instruments, not a new rule or lint class.\nOpen point the fixer must close first: where cloud-sessions.tsv is written and what its created_at column holds were not found; confirm the record's shape before writing the detector.\nCost: S-M, about 40-80 lines plus a fixture with a null control (a seat that did return must not be reported). If the tick line is wanted in orchestrator_tick.py, that part belongs to R10-FLY-CAP-LINE.",
+    "Triage (s4, 2026-10-23 07:10Z, tick_loop.sh stale checkout, accepted, narrowed), verbatim:\nDetect that the checkout is behind origin/main and restart between passes; never fast-forward in place under a running relay.\n\nMeasured facts: #2626's head moved 4 times because a 30 h loop ran a stale cloud_relay.sh; this is the second instance of the class (P4, 2026-10-21, was closed as \"restart after a merge that touches tick_loop.sh or cloud_relay.sh\"), and the recurrence that decision named. A fast-forward of the checkout each pass is unsound at origin/main tools/audit/seat/tick_loop.sh: the loop is a bash script read incrementally and it detaches cloud_relay.sh (RELAY_HOLDER, header lines 1-22), so rewriting either file while it runs corrupts the pass or a relay in flight, and a dirty or branch-checked-out tree makes the ff fail or move a branch. R10-ORCH-MACHINERY, the earlier candidate, is absorbed and is not valid.\nMechanism: each pass, `git fetch -q origin main` (git, not API), count `git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/main -- tools/audit/seat tools/pr tools/audit/carry_audit.py` (only the paths the loop runs); above 0 print a `TICK-STALE <utc> behind=<n>` banner on the pass line; above a bound N (default 25 commits, or any change to tick_loop.sh or cloud_relay.sh themselves) and with no relay in flight (the flock is free) and a clean detached tree, `git checkout -q --detach origin/main` and exec the new tick_loop.sh with the same args; otherwise refuse the pass with rc and the banner. The restart point is between passes, never mid-relay.\nAlternatives rejected: R10-FLOW-AUTOTRAIN (M), R10-ORCH-BEATD (L; its supervised beat may replace the loop, so the check moves with the loop and BEATD's brief should say so). This group already edits relay_start in tick_loop.sh (P15), so the check lands in the same file without a second PR.\nCost: S-M, about 30-50 lines in tick_loop.sh plus self-test rows with a stub git: behind-by-0 passes silently; behind with a tick_loop.sh change and no relay in flight restarts; a relay in flight (lock held) does not restart; a dirty tree refuses and prints the banner, never ff. One mutant per row (file-scoped ids tick_loop-N)."
